faqs:
  - q: "How much will my child's education cost in 15 years?"
    a: >-
      Multiply today's fees by education inflation. A course costing ₹20 lakh today, inflating at 10% a year, costs roughly ₹83.5 lakh in 15 years — more than four times the present figure. At 8% it would be about ₹63.4 lakh. This is why planning from today's sticker price badly understates what you need.
  - q: "What education inflation rate should I use for India?"
    a: >-
      Higher than headline inflation. General CPI inflation in India has averaged around 5–6% in recent years, but tuition fees at private colleges have typically risen faster, and 8–10% is the range most planners use. Overseas education carries the additional risk of rupee depreciation on top of local fee increases.
  - q: "Where should I invest for a child's education?"
    a: >-
      It depends almost entirely on how far away the goal is. More than seven years out, diversified equity funds are the usual choice because they have the time to recover from a bad stretch. Inside three years, the money belongs in debt funds, RDs or FDs — a 30% fall in the final year cannot be made up. Many parents shift gradually from equity to debt over the last three to four years.
  - q: "Is Sukanya Samriddhi Yojana good for a daughter's education?"
    a: >-
      It is a sound, government-backed option with tax-free returns, and the rate has historically run above PPF. The trade-offs are that it is restricted to a daughter under 10, locks money until she is 21 with only partial withdrawal allowed at 18, and pays a fixed rate that may trail equity over fifteen-plus years. Many families use it alongside equity SIPs rather than instead of them.
  - q: "Should I take an education loan instead of saving?"
    a: >-
      They are complements, not alternatives. Education loans in India carry interest of roughly 9–12%, and Section 80E allows a deduction on the interest paid for up to eight years under the old regime. Saving early reduces how much you need to borrow, which is what makes the loan manageable. Starting a SIP fifteen years ahead is far cheaper than servicing a loan for eight years afterwards.
  - q: "How much should I invest every month for my child's education?"
    a: >-
      For a goal of ₹83.5 lakh in 15 years at a 12% return, the SIP works out to about ₹16,600 a month. The same target over 10 years needs roughly ₹35,900 — more than double, for only five fewer years. Every year of delay raises the figure sharply, which is the single strongest argument for starting while the child is young.
