<article class="article">
  <h2>Why education needs its own calculator</h2>
  <p>Most goals can be planned with general inflation. Education cannot. College and professional-course fees in India have historically risen faster than the consumer price index, and the gap compounds: at 10% a year, fees double roughly every seven years, so a child born today may face four times the present cost by the time they reach university. Parents who plan from today's fee schedule routinely find themselves short by a factor of two or more.</p>

  <h2>How it is calculated</h2>
  <div class="formula">Future cost = today's cost × (1 + education inflation)<sup>years until the course starts</sup></div>
  <p>Anything you have already set aside is grown at your expected return and subtracted, and the remaining shortfall is converted into the monthly SIP that closes it in the time available:</p>
  <div class="formula">SIP = shortfall ÷ [ ((1 + i)<sup>n</sup> − 1) ÷ i × (1 + i) ]</div>

  <h2>Typical course costs in India</h2>
  <p>Indicative present-day ranges for the full course, to use as a starting point. Fees vary enormously between government and private institutions, so replace these with the actual figures for the colleges you have in mind:</p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Course</th><th>Typical total cost today</th><th>At 10% inflation, in 15 years</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Engineering (private)</th><td>₹8–20 lakh</td><td>₹33–84 lakh</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Medicine (private MBBS)</th><td>₹50 lakh–1 crore</td><td>₹2.1–4.2 crore</td></tr>
        <tr><th>MBA (top-tier Indian)</th><td>₹20–30 lakh</td><td>₹84 lakh–1.25 crore</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Undergraduate abroad</th><td>₹60 lakh–1.5 crore</td><td>₹2.5–6.3 crore</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="chart-note">Indicative ranges only, covering tuition plus living costs where applicable. Overseas figures carry additional currency risk.</p>

  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>Your child is 3, and you expect them to start a course at 18 — fifteen years away. A degree costing ₹20 lakh today, with education inflation at 10%, will cost about <strong>₹83.5 lakh</strong> when they get there. With nothing saved yet and a 12% expected return, you would need a SIP of roughly <strong>₹16,600 a month</strong>.</p>
  <p>Wait until the child is 8 and you have ten years instead of fifteen. The course then costs about ₹51.9 lakh — less, because there is less time for fees to inflate — yet the SIP required jumps to roughly ₹22,300 a month, because your money has five fewer years to compound. Delay is expensive in both directions.</p>

  <h2>Matching the investment to the timeline</h2>
  <p>The right place for this money changes as the goal approaches, and getting that wrong is the most common way education plans fail:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>More than 10 years away.</strong> Diversified equity funds, with the bulk in index or flexi-cap funds. There is ample time to absorb a poor stretch.</li>
    <li><strong>5 to 10 years.</strong> Still equity-led, but start building a debt component. A hybrid or balanced-advantage fund suits parents who would rather not manage the shift themselves.</li>
    <li><strong>3 to 5 years.</strong> Move decisively toward debt. Protecting what you have accumulated now matters more than growing it further.</li>
    <li><strong>Under 3 years.</strong> Debt funds, RDs or FDs only. A market fall in the final year cannot be recovered before the fees are due.</li>
    <li><strong>Shift gradually, not in one move.</strong> Transferring a fixed amount each month over the last three years avoids betting the whole outcome on the level of the market on a single day.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For a broader goal with any timeline, the <a href="/goal-planner/">goal planner</a> applies the same logic and suggests an asset mix for the horizon you enter.</p>
</article>

<script>
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
  function recalc() {
    var cage = Math.round(CM.val('cage'));
    var sage = Math.round(CM.val('sage'));
    var cost = CM.val('cost');
    var einf = CM.val('einf') / 100;
    var r = CM.val('r') / 100;
    var have = CM.val('have');

    var n = sage - cage;
    if (n <= 0) {
      CM.set('out-fc', '—');
      CM.set('out-fc-sub', 'Course start age must be above the child’s current age');
      ['out-yrs', 'out-mult', 'out-sip'].forEach(function (id) { CM.set(id, '—'); });
      return;
    }

    var fc = cost * Math.pow(1 + einf, n);
    var haveFv = have * Math.pow(1 + r, n);
    var gap = Math.max(0, fc - haveFv);
    var i = r / 12, months = n * 12;
    var sip = i === 0 ? gap / months : gap / (((Math.pow(1 + i, months) - 1) / i) * (1 + i));

    CM.set('out-fc', CM.inr(fc));
    var w = CM.words(fc);
    CM.set('out-fc-sub', (w ? 'about ' + w + ' · ' : '') + 'when the child turns ' + sage);
    CM.set('out-yrs', n + (n === 1 ? ' year' : ' years'));
    CM.set('out-mult', (Math.round((fc / cost) * 10) / 10) + '×');
    CM.set('out-sip', gap <= 0 ? 'Already funded ✓' : CM.inr(sip));
    CM.set('out-sip-sub', gap <= 0 ? 'existing savings cover the cost' : 'for ' + n + ' years at ' + CM.num(CM.val('r'), 1) + '%');

    var labels = [], costSeries = [], fundSeries = [], rows = [];
    var bal = have;
    for (var y = 0; y <= n; y++) {
      labels.push('Age ' + (cage + y));
      costSeries.push(cost * Math.pow(1 + einf, y));
      fundSeries.push(bal);
      rows.push([cage + y, CM.inr(cost * Math.pow(1 + einf, y)), CM.inr(bal)]);
      if (y < n) bal = bal * (1 + r) + sip * 12 * (1 + r);
    }
    CM.lineChart('chart-edu', labels, [
      { label: 'Course cost', data: costSeries, role: '--s3' },
      { label: 'Your fund', data: fundSeries, role: '--s1', fill: true }
    ]);
    CM.table('sched-out', ["Child's age", 'Course cost then', 'Fund value'], rows);
  }
  CM.bind(['cage', 'sage', 'cost', 'einf', 'r', 'have'], recalc);
});
</script>
